PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Current belief: A. Entrechat six remains the best answer under standard classical ballet terminology. Core reason: an even-numbered entrechat, including entrechat six, begins in fifth position and returns to the same fifth-position front/back alignment—same foot in front. The main counterargument in round 3 is that entrechat six has six leg movements but three beats/crossings, an odd number, so it changes feet. I still think this is a counting-unit error: the “three beats” are paired accents/classroom shorthand, not three independent changes of fifth alignment; otherwise even-numbered entrechats would effectively be change steps, which is not standard classification.

D. Glissade derrière is the main rival. Many colleagues shifted to D (1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 10, 12, 15, 16, 17) arguing it begins in fifth, back foot glides back, front foot closes in front, preserving the original front/back relation. However, this is not a clean structural guarantee: glissade derrière is a traveling/linking step, and its closing front/back relationship can depend on working foot and context; it may change feet in standard execution. Thus it is less definitionally reliable than entrechat six.

A holdouts (2, 5, 8, 9, 11, 13, 14, 18) defend the even-numbered entrechat parity argument. B is explicitly changé, C varies by dessus/dessous, E involves rond-de-jambe/change action. Unresolved: if an exam source uses the “three beats = odd” convention for entrechat six, it might favor D; but standard ballet vocabulary favors A. My current vote remains A.}

